.TH UMEM_DEBUG 3MALLOC "Jul 26, 2002"
.SH NAME
umem_debug \- debugging features of the umem library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.LP
.nf
\fBcc\fR [ \fIflag\fR\&.\|.\|. ] \fIfile\fR\&.\|.\|. \fB-lumem\fR [ \fIlibrary\fR\&.\|.\|. ]
#include <\fBumem.h\fR>
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
.LP
The \fBlibumem\fR library provides debugging features that detect memory leaks,
buffer overruns, multiple frees, use of uninitialized data, use of freed data,
and many other common programming errors. The activation of the run-time
debugging features is controlled by environment variables.
.sp
.LP
When the library detects an error, it writes a description of the error to an
internal buffer that is readable with the \fBumem_inspect\fR(3) API
(also surfaced via the \fBumem\fR(1) command and the
\fB::umem_status\fR \fBmdb\fR(1) \fIdcmd\fR on Solaris) and then
calls \fBabort\fR(3C).
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBUMEM_DEBUG\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 16n
This variable contains a list of comma-separated options.  Unrecognized options
are ignored. Possible options include:
.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBaudit\fR[=\fIframes\fR]\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
Enables transaction auditing (UMF_AUDIT flag). Records detailed information for
each allocation and free operation:
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Thread ID that performed the operation
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
High-resolution timestamp
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Stack trace with configurable depth
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Pointer to previous transaction log entry
.RE
.sp
The \fIframes\fR parameter sets the number of stack frames recorded in the
auditing structure. The upper bound for frames is implementation-defined. If a
larger value is requested, the upper bound is used instead.
.sp
If \fIframes\fR is not specified or is not an integer, the default value of 15
is used.
.sp
This option also enables the \fBguards\fR option automatically. If transaction
logging (see \fBUMEM_LOGGING\fR) is enabled, auditing information is logged to
the transaction log for later analysis.
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=audit=30 ./myapp    # Record 30 stack frames per transaction
UMEM_DEBUG=audit ./myapp       # Use default 15 frames
.fi
.in -2
.sp
Audit mode is essential for debugging memory leaks and use-after-free errors.
Use with debugger extensions (see \fBDebugger Extensions\fR) to analyze
allocation history.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBcontents\fR[=\fIcount\fR]\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
Enables freed-buffer content logging (UMF_CONTENTS flag). When auditing is also
enabled, the first \fIcount\fR bytes of each buffer are saved when freed. This
allows post-mortem analysis of buffer contents at the time of deallocation.
.sp
If a buffer is shorter than \fIcount\fR bytes, it is logged in its entirety.
If \fIcount\fR is not specified or is not an integer, the default value of 256
is used.
.sp
Content logging requires both auditing and contents logging to be enabled in
UMEM_LOGGING (see below). The saved contents are accessible through debugger
extensions or can be dumped using the contents log.
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=audit,contents=512 \\
UMEM_LOGGING=transaction=1M,contents=10M \\
./myapp
.fi
.in -2
.sp
This is useful for debugging corruption issues where the buffer contents at
free time provide clues about the source of corruption.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBdefault\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
This option is equivalent to \fBaudit\fR,\fBcontents\fR,\fBguards\fR.
Enables comprehensive debugging with transaction auditing, content logging,
and buffer guards. This is the recommended setting for development and testing.
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=default ./myapp
.fi
.in -2
.sp
Performance impact: 50-70% overhead compared to production builds. Memory
overhead: 100-500 bytes per allocation for audit structures and guards.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBguards\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
Enables buffer guards and pattern fills (UMF_DEADBEEF and UMF_REDZONE flags).
This mode helps detect:
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Use of uninitialized data
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Use of freed memory (use-after-free)
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Buffer overruns (via redzone)
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Buffer underruns (via pattern verification)
.RE
.sp
When enabled, \fBguards\fR performs the following actions:
.sp
\fBUMF_DEADBEEF pattern fill:\fR
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Freed buffers are filled with \fB0xdeadbeef\fR pattern
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
On allocation, the pattern is verified to detect use-after-free
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Verified buffers are then filled with \fB0xbaddcafe\fR (uninitialized pattern)
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Application can detect use of uninitialized memory in debugging tools
.RE
.sp
\fBUMF_REDZONE protection:\fR
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
8-byte redzone added after each buffer
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Redzone filled with \fB0xfeedfacefeedfaceULL\fR pattern
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Redzone verified on every allocation and free operation
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Corruption triggers immediate abort with diagnostic message
.RE
.sp
For caches with constructors or destructors, \fBguards\fR mode disables
object caching. The constructor is called on every allocation and the
destructor on every free. This ensures:
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Buffers are always in the constructed state when allocated
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
\fBassert\fR(3C) statements in destructors can verify proper object state
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Objects returned in improper state are detected immediately
.RE
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=guards ./myapp
# Buffer overrun will cause:
# umem: redzone violation: buffer 0x7f1234 overwritten
# umem: previous allocation at [stack trace]
# Aborted (core dumped)
.fi
.in -2
.sp
Performance impact: 10-20% overhead. Guards mode is lightweight enough for
continuous use during development.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBverbose\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
The library writes error descriptions to standard error before aborting. These
messages are not localized. Use this option to see diagnostic information
immediately without examining core dumps or debugger output.
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=guards,verbose ./myapp
.fi
.in -2
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBlite\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 20n
Enables lightweight debugging mode (UMF_LITE flag). This mode provides a
subset of debugging features with lower overhead than full audit mode:
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Buffer tagging without full stack traces
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Reduced memory overhead per allocation
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Faster than full audit mode but still catches most bugs
.RE
.sp
Lite mode is automatically enabled for buffers meeting these criteria:
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Size is at least \fBumem_lite_minsize\fR (default: 0 bytes)
.RE
.RS +4
.TP
.ie t \(bu
.el o
Alignment is at most \fBumem_lite_maxalign\fR (default: 1024 bytes)
.RE
.sp
For buffers outside these parameters, full audit mode (if enabled) is used.
.sp
\fBExample:\fR
.sp
.in +2
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=lite ./myapp
.fi
.in -2
.sp
Lite mode is useful for performance-sensitive applications that still need
some debugging capability. Performance impact: 15-25% overhead (vs 50-70%
for full default mode).
.RE

.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBUMEM_LOGGING\fR\fR
.ad
.RS 16n
To be enabled, this variable should be set to a comma-separated list of
in-memory logs. The logs available are:
.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBtransaction\fR[=\fIsize\fR]\fR
.ad
.RS 22n
If the \fBaudit\fR debugging option is set (see \fBUMEM_DEBUG\fR), the audit
structures from previous transactions are entered into this log.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBcontents\fR[=\fIsize\fR]\fR
.ad
.RS 22n
If the \fBaudit\fR debugging option is set, the contents of objects are
recorded in this log as they are freed.
.sp
If the "contents" debugging option was not set, 256 bytes of each freed buffer
are saved.
.RE

.sp
.ne 2
.na
\fB\fBfail\fR[=\fIsize\fR]\fR
.ad
.RS 22n
Records are entered into this log for every failed allocation.
.RE

For any of these options, if \fIsize\fR is not specified, the default value of
64k is used.  The \fIsize\fR parameter must be an integer that can be qualified
with K, M, G, or T to specify kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes, or terabytes,
respectively.
.sp
Logs that are not listed or that have either a size of 0 or an invalid size are
disabled.
.sp
The log is disabled if during initialization the requested amount of storage
cannot be allocated.
.RE

